judgments.calibrationWith100Usd:
Live micro-trading is the only ground truth for execution realism, and the plan needs it early. Do not wait for the Rust V2 adapter (M8).

Probe. Early, in parallel with steps 2-6, the user runs a small standalone calibration probe on the official CLOB V2 client. It is not the TS trading-bot, and the agent never places orders. Safeguards:
- a hard total budget cap;
- a per-order cap of about the minimum size, roughly 5 shares;
- a kill switch and a heartbeat;
- BTC 15m markets only.

Experiments:
- post-only at the best bid, joining an existing level;
- post-only improving the spread by one tick, so queue-ahead is 0;
- small crossing FAK/FOK, to measure the taker delay, slippage and the real fee against the market's feeSchedule;
- timed cancels, to separate the place, cancel and fill-notification legs.

Data. The probe journals intent, ack, fill and cancel times with the raw REST and user-WS payloads. worker-2 records the same markets with Recorder V4, including trade prints.

Comparison. The Rust sim replays the same intents at the same times on those recordings, with our own order sizes subtracted from the recorded book. Compare fill or no-fill, time to fill, size, fee and latency.

Expected yield:
- Good estimates: latency distributions, the taker delay, fee correctness and slippage.
- Only a rough check: maker fill and queue position, at a few hundred orders.

Uses beyond calibration:
- The payloads become fixtures for the Rust V2 adapter.
- Later, the activated Rust live engine's journals give a second, larger calibration set at strategy level.
- Every calibration result is reported with confidence intervals and never used to tune toward optimism. Worst-queue stays the conservative bound.
